A||||3||Ed||Autocross classes||||1||
Z||000000||Ed||02-11-2001||11:24 PM||||The February issue of "Grassroots Motorsports" has the results of the SCCA Solo 2 autocross races held in Topeka, Kansas.  Two 88 Fieros were well placed in the E modified class.  Total times were very low, approaching some of the open wheel cars.<P>Questions:  Are the total times comparable for all the classes?  That is, did the Fieros really go faster than most of the other cars? And what modifications are allowed in E modified?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Greg Piet||02-12-2001||06:03 AM||||OK to start things off<BR>BOTH of those Fieros were not really Fieros (according to my sources named below).<BR>About the only things stock on at least one of those is the shifter and the rear tail lights(believe me I have checked).<P>One of those EM cars is called a Fiero "tomcat" by its owner which has a tubular frame, all custom panels and an insane V8 pumping out a MINIMUM of 500 hp.  The one I am referring to is raced here in las vegas at every autocross by our autocross chairman Rick Kostelas.<P>I was not at nationals so From now on I am going by information given to me from Dave Hironaka the F125 national champ.<P>The other Fiero in EM is similiar to the one mentioned above but has about 800 hp.<P>Doing something like putting a turbo in your stock v6 apparently will put you in the same class as these cars.  I beleive a 3.4 upgrade and lower would NOT put you in EM.<P>If you wanna stay in stock or street prepared then no internal motor mods.  I have been told that the Fiero is not a competitive national level autocrosser in CS or CSP due to its weight (frame).  I would definately like to prove them wrong, but I am not a good enough driver to spank everyone in my region, let alone the best in the country.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR>Bring it to the track.<BR>Lets see if all those mods really helped!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||02-12-2001||12:27 PM||||what about wheel/tire combos? if i put some larger wheels on for looks and performance do i have to run a stock size (mine came with 14's and has 16's on it now) to be with what ever the beginners class would be?<P>i've never done any kind of racing, but this scca thing looks kind of fun. but i don't know if something i've done for looks or performance would put me out of the rank amateur class that i would definitly need to be in. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Greg Piet||02-12-2001||08:45 PM||||Yes non stock size rims take you out of the stock category.<BR>In local areas most people dont care about such things.<BR>Officially you can always disclose such things to your competitors and if they dont have a problem everything is peachy.<P>There is also a STS class which is supposed to be for street tire cars, but with 4 doors.<BR>My region added the Fiero to the list of allowed cars, but I never ran in that class.<P>I think in street prepared any size rim is allowed.  General idea of Street prepared mods...<BR>suspension/intake/exhaust/bushings(except metal)/wheels/linkage/body parts such as non stock wings, air dams, etc.<P>Just go out and have fun to start.  There is no novice class in my region, and face it you are going to get spanked by seasoned drivers, but the learning curve CAN be pretty steep to be relatively close to the seasoned drivers times.  Then getting to the skill to beat the seasoned drivrs takes a long time, and all the mods wont save you, its just seat time.<P>Most people in the SCCA are really nice and will help you out if you are friendly and ask.  Now when they are walking the track trying to visualize the course, is not a good time to ask questions, but alot of people walk the track in a group to start and discuss what lines to run.<BR>It is a blast even if you lose!<P>I have seen rookies come out in brand new vettes/Honda S2000's and they have to compete against national level competitors but they still have fun which is the main goal.||||reg||1
